---
/**
 * Get Help — the first nav slot.
 *
 * On the legacy site the page carrying this responsibility ("Veteran and Family
 * Support") had a body consisting of exactly one link. The three resources a
 * struggling veteran would actually want were filed under three unrelated
 * sections: the Vet Center under "Community Support", the Treatment Docket
 * under a top-level item called "Fairfax Court", and the chapter's own
 * financial assistance under a page called "Chapter 227 Key Mission".
 *
 * The single biggest content gap on the whole site surfaces here: nowhere does
 * it say how a veteran actually ASKS for help. That is flagged, not invented.
 */
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import Card from '../../components/Card.astro';
import CardGrid from '../../components/CardGrid.astro';
import ReviewNote from '../../components/ReviewNote.astro';
import ContactList from '../../components/ContactList.astro';
import { currency } from '../../lib/format';
import site from '../../data/site.json';
import impact from '../../data/impact.json';

const latest = impact.years[0];

const helpWith = [
  'Rent and mortgage payments',
  'Utility bills',
  'Car payments and car repairs',
  'Medical expenses',
  'Back-to-school costs for children',
  'Holiday gifts for families',
];
---

<PageLayout
  title="Get Help"
  description="Chapter 227 provides direct financial assistance to veterans and their families in Northern Virginia, and can refer you to counseling, housing and treatment court support."
  eyebrow="For veterans and families"
  lede="If you served, and things are tight, the chapter may be able to help — and if we cannot, we probably know who can."
>
  <!-- Crisis first, before anything else on the page. -->
  <section class="urgent">
    <h2 class="urgent__title">If you are in crisis right now</h2>
    <p class="urgent__body">
      Call the <strong>Veterans Crisis Line</strong> — dial <a href="tel:988">988</a>, then press <strong>1</strong>.
      You can also text <a href="sms:838255">838255</a> or
      <a href={site.crisis.url} rel="noopener">chat online</a>. It is staffed 24 hours a day, every day, by people
      who understand. You do not need to be enrolled in VA care.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="stack">
    <h2>What the chapter can help with</h2>
    <p>
      Chapter 227 gives money directly to veterans and their families in Northern Virginia. This is not a loan and it
      is not a grant program with forms and a review board — it is one veteran organization deciding to cover
      something that has to be covered. In {latest.year} the chapter paid out {currency(latest.totalSupport)} this way.
    </p>

    <ul class="pills">
      {helpWith.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
    </ul>

    <p class="muted">
      The chapter has also paid for things that fit no category at all — a security deposit that let a veteran move
      into his own apartment, and cancer treatment costs for the wife of an Army Ranger.
    </p>

    <ReviewNote topic="How to ask for help">
      <p>
        <strong>The current website never explains how a veteran actually requests assistance.</strong> It describes
        what the chapter pays for and how much it spent, but gives no intake route — no form, no phone number for
        assistance specifically, and no named person to ask.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the most consequential gap found in the whole content audit. The chapter needs to decide who takes
        these requests and how, and that route belongs at the top of this page.
      </p>
      <p>
        A Foundation newsletter forwarded to the site in 2026 describes the program differently from this page. It
        says requests arrive as <strong>referrals</strong> from partner agencies &mdash; the Virginia Department of
        Veterans Services, county veterans offices, Veterans Treatment Docket mentors, the VFW and the American
        Legion; that a <strong>Financial Assistance Committee</strong> documents and verifies each one; and that
        payment goes to the landlord, utility company or repair shop, <strong>never to the veteran in cash</strong>.
        This page says close to the opposite in two places: that the chapter gives money directly to veterans and
        their families, and that this is not a grant program with forms and a review board.
      </p>
      <p>
        Both descriptions cannot be right, and that newsletter is a fundraising appeal rather than the
        chapter&rsquo;s intake policy &mdash; so this page has not been rewritten around it. The chapter needs to
        confirm which description is correct, and in particular
        <strong>whether a veteran with no caseworker may ask the chapter directly</strong>. If a partner referral
        is required, this page must say so plainly and name the first door to knock on.
        <a href="/give/nvvvf">About the Foundation &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </ReviewNote>

    <h3>Ask the chapter</h3>
    <p>
      Until a dedicated intake route exists, the chapter&rsquo;s general line is the way in.
    </p>
    <ContactList only={['general', 'treatment-court']} />
  </section>

  <section class="stack">
    <h2>Other places that can help</h2>
    <CardGrid min="18rem">
      <Card title="Alexandria Vet Center" href="/get-help/vet-center" eyebrow="Counseling">
        <p>
          Free, confidential counseling for combat veterans and their families — readjustment, PTSD, bereavement.
          Open weekdays, with evening and weekend appointments on request.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card title="Veterans Treatment Court" href="/get-help/treatment-court" eyebrow="Legal">
        <p>
          If you are facing charges in Fairfax County connected to addiction or PTSD, the Veterans Treatment Docket
          offers treatment and a veteran mentor rather than a jail sentence.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card title="The Lamb Center" eyebrow="Food, clothing, shelter">
        <p>
          A day shelter in Fairfax for homeless men and women, many of them veterans, providing clothing, food and
          daily necessities. The chapter&rsquo;s AVVA members collect for it at every monthly meeting.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card title="VA Supportive Housing (VASH)" href="/programs" eyebrow="Housing">
        <p>
          About half of everything the chapter spends goes to families in the VASH program, through preloaded debit
          cards distributed by VASH caseworkers at Christmas and back-to-school.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </CardGrid>
  </section>
</PageLayout>

<style>
  .urgent {
    margin-block-end: var(--s-7);
    padding: var(--s-5) var(--s-6);
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-crisis) 7%, var(--c-surface));
    border: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-crisis) 28%, var(--c-border));
    border-inline-start: 4px solid var(--c-crisis);
    border-radius: var(--r-md);
  }

  .urgent__title {
    font-size: var(--t-lg);
    color: var(--c-crisis);
  }

  .urgent__body {
    margin-block-start: var(--s-3);
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  section + section {
    margin-block-start: var(--s-8);
  }

  .pills {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--s-2);
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  .pills li {
    padding: var(--s-2) var(--s-4);
    background: var(--c-primary-tint);
    border-radius: 100px;
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--c-primary-dark);
  }

  h3 {
    margin-block-start: var(--s-6);
  }
</style>
